1: # The actual exterior slope and the conifold Euler cube
2: 
3: This working proof computes the exterior slope for the actual
4: semifree fixed-sphere model.  It retains the tubular framing: its
5: integer is the Euler cube in that same framing.  Comparing a Cox
6: product marking with the descended rotating marking of P3K.9 is a
7: further explicit marking calculation, not an automatic identification.
8: 
9: ## 1. The actual spaces and their integral cohomology
10: 
11: Let \(X\cong S^6\) have the actual semifree delta action with
12: fixed complex curve \(F\cong S^2\) and normal weights \((-1,+1)\).
13: Let \(Y=X/S^1\cong S^5\), remove the fixed tube, and write
14: \[
15:  E=X\setminus\operatorname{int}\nu F,
16:  \qquad P=Y\setminus\operatorname{int}\nu F,
17:  \qquad E\longrightarrow P.
18: \]
19: The last map is the actual principal delta-circle bundle.  Its
20: integral Euler class \(x\) evaluates one on the positive Hopf
21: meridian.  Alexander duality gives
22: \(H^*(P;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z\) in degrees zero and two and
23: zero otherwise.  The primitive meridian identifies
24: \(H^2(P;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Zx\).
25: 
26: Fix a tube product for the physical return, and make the return the
27: identity on that tube by its specified collar isotopy.  Put hats on
28: the physical positive mapping tori.  The quotient return preserves
29: \(x\).  The integral Wang sequence therefore gives
30: \[
31:  H^1(\widehat P)=\mathbb Z\theta,
32:  \quad H^2(\widehat P)=\mathbb Zx,
33:  \quad H^3(\widehat P)=\mathbb Z(x\theta),
34:  \quad H^4(\widehat P)=0.
35: \]
36: The circle bundle \(\widehat E\to\widehat P\) has the same
37: actual Euler class.  Its Gysin sequence gives an isomorphism
38: \[
39:  \pi_*:H^3(\widehat E;\mathbb Z)\longrightarrow
40:        H^2(\widehat P;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Zx.             \tag{EVar.1}
41: \]
42: Indeed the preceding term is the quotient of
43: \(H^3(\widehat P)\) by
44: \(xH^1(\widehat P)\), which is zero, and the following
45: \(H^4(\widehat P)\) is zero.  Thus no rational-lattice ambiguity
46: is present in the slope.
47: 
48: Let \(h\) integrate one on the complex-oriented \(F\), and
49: let \(s\) integrate one on the normal \(S^3\) oriented as the
50: boundary of the original complex normal two-plane.  The chosen
51: tubular framing identifies
52: \[
53:  \partial\widehat E=F\times S^3\times S^1_\theta,
54:  \qquad H^3(\partial\widehat E)
55:         =\mathbb Zs\oplus\mathbb Z(h\theta).
56: \]
57: By (EVar.1), the image of restriction is generated by
58: \(s+t h\theta\) for a unique integer \(t\).
59: 
60: ## 2. The relative primitive that measures the slope
61: 
62: Complete \(\widehat P\) by the actual conifold cap
63: \(D^3_F\times S^2_{\rm mer}\times S^1_\theta\), using the
64: same tubular framing.  Call the closed six-manifold \(N_{\rm phys}\).
65: The actual line extends over this cap by the meridian line.  Choose
66: a closed real two-form representing its integral class \(x\) that
67: is exactly the meridian form on a collar of the cap and throughout
68: the cap.  Choose a principal connection \(\eta\) on
69: \(\widehat E\), normalized by positive delta fibre integral one,
70: with \(d\eta=\pi^*x\).  The connection may be chosen to be the
71: positive Hopf connection on the boundary product, since the actual
72: boundary line is the meridian line.  Its possible parameter gauge
73: extends over the cap by P3K.7.
74: 
75: Since \(H^4(\widehat P;\mathbb R)=0\), choose
76: \(\beta\in\Omega^3(\widehat P)\) with
77: \(d\beta=x^2\).  Then
78: \[
79:                  \alpha=-\eta\wedge\pi^*x+\pi^*\beta
80:                                                                \tag{EVar.2}
81: \]
82: is closed.  Fibre integration gives \(-x\), so (EVar.1) proves
83: that its cohomology class is the corresponding integral generator.
84: Changing \(\beta\) by a closed form changes it by a multiple of
85: \(x\theta\) in cohomology.  Its pullback is zero in
86: \(H^3(\widehat E)\), so the resulting generator is independent
87: of that choice.
88: 
89: The normal orientation matters.  Conjugating the negative-weight
90: normal coordinate converts the action into positive Hopf action
91: and reverses the normal real four-plane orientation.  Hence the
92: original complex normal \(S^3\) is the negative of the positive
93: Hopf \(S^3\).  The integral of \(\eta\wedge x\) over the former
94: is minus one.  Therefore (EVar.2) has normal period plus one.
95: On the cycle \(F\times\{p\}\times S^1_\theta\), its first
96: term vanishes because \(x\) is the meridian form.  Consequently
97: \[
98:              t=\int_{F\times S^1_\theta}\beta.          \tag{EVar.3}
99: \]
100: This is the exact relative primitive that the absolute cohomology
101: of the exterior by itself does not determine.
102: 
103: ## 3. Stokes and the two return conventions
104: 
105: The quotient convention already fixed in P3K.7 gives
106: \(\Omega_Y=\Omega_F\wedge\Omega_{\nu,\mathrm{Hopf}}\).
107: Thus the boundary of the complement \(P\) is
108: \(-F\times S^2_{\rm mer}\).  The physical mapping torus is
109: oriented by fibre followed by positive parameter, so
110: \[
111:  \partial\widehat P
112:        =-F\times S^2_{\rm mer}\times S^1_\theta.
113: \]
114: The meridian form has square zero on the product cap, and therefore
115: its cube integrates to zero there.  Stokes on \(\widehat P\)
116: gives, with the displayed orientation,
117: \[
118:  \begin{aligned}
119:  k_{\rm phys}
120:    &=\int_{N_{\rm phys}}x^3
121:      =\int_{\widehat P}d(\beta\wedge x)\\
122:    &=\int_{\partial\widehat P}\beta\wedge x
123:      =-\int_{F\times S^1_\theta}\beta=-t.
124:  \end{aligned}                                                   \tag{EVar.4}
125: \]
126: The physical positive family is \(M_{f^{-1}}\) in Jin's convention,
127: exactly as in P3K.7.  Hence \(k_{\rm Jin}(f)=-k_{\rm phys}\), and
128: the actual signed relation is
129: \[
130:                          t=k_{\rm Jin}(f).             \tag{EVar.5}
131: \]
132: It is a calculation from the actual principal bundle and the actual
133: conifold cap.  It does not assume that the exterior family is a
134: product relative to its boundary.
135: 
136: ## 4. What is and is not fixed by this calculation
137: 
138: In the descended rotating tubular framing of P3K.9, the original
139: positive return has \(k_{\rm Jin}=-1\), so its exterior slope is
140: \(t=-1\).  On its double parameter cover the slope is \(-2\),
141: by pulling back (EVar.3).  On the one-gamma sixfold cover it is
142: \(-6\).  These statements use that same rotating boundary product.
143: 
144: A Cox product marking may differ from it.  The identity (EVar.5)
145: holds again in the Cox marking, with the conifold Euler cube formed
146: using that marking; it does not equate its value to the rotating
147: value before the marking map is calculated.  Changing the section
148: \(F\times S^1\to F\times S^3\times S^1\) by a degree-\(d\)
149: normal map changes the second period of the generator by \(d\),
150: and the same Stokes calculation changes its conifold Euler cube
151: by \(d\).  Thus both sides retain precisely the same framing
152: ambiguity.
153: 
154: For a flop of another, non-fixed critical curve, the delta quotient
155: may have finite stabilizers; the principal-bundle argument above is
156: not silently applied there.  The general-fan relative Pontryagin
157: comparison still needs its own actual transport to the chosen
158: curve and its Cox product marking.
